destination: Nix development shells run as first-class sandboxed environments on Linux and macOS, confined in user space by bubblewrap and Seatbelt respectively, with no virtualisation
created: 2026-10-31
tickets[8]{id,question,blockers,state,answer,evidence}:
  T-001,"Can App Sandbox confine an unsigned devShell binary?",,answered,"No. App Sandbox policy is entitlement-derived and only applies to a signed bundle; the Darwin spike could not use it",evidence/T-001.md
  T-002,"Does Seatbelt confine an unsigned binary on a current macOS?",,answered,"Yes. macOS 27.2, adhoc-signed binary, write outside the workspace returns EPERM; AC-011 proven",evidence/T-002.md
  T-003,"Is bwrap viable as an unprivileged Linux sandbox?",,answered,"Yes. It builds the sandbox from unprivileged user namespaces, with no root and no daemon",evidence/T-003.md
  T-004,"Can the host Nix store be shared without copying?",,answered,"Yes. Store paths are content-addressed, so a read-only bind exposes the same paths with the same meaning",evidence/T-004.md
  T-005,"Can bwrap restrict egress by destination?",,answered,"No. An unshared network namespace has loopback only; host filtering needs a proxy",evidence/T-005.md
  T-006,"Does confinement require virtualisation?",,answered,"No. Both mechanisms enforce in the shared kernel",evidence/T-006.md
  T-007,"Can lifecycle work without a supervisor?",,answered,"Yes. Process groups give pause, resume and kill. A setsid escape stays confined but cannot be addressed",evidence/T-007.md
  T-008,"Do the target distributions permit unprivileged user namespaces?",,open,,
decision: "Adopt bubblewrap on Linux and Seatbelt on macOS. Record explicitly that macOS App Sandbox requires a signed bundle, so unsigned devShell binaries use the deprecated sandbox_init path; its longevity is a carried risk. Spike bubblewrap first in ADR 2026-10-31-linux-bwrap-sandbox; its portability matrix must answer T-008 before a production spec is written. Further carried risks: egress beyond loopback needs a proxy on both platforms, and Linux per-command cost is unmeasured until the spike runs."
adr: adrs/2026-10-31-linux-bwrap-sandbox
handedOver: adrs/2026-10-31-linux-bwrap-sandbox (probe for T-008)
steps[6]:
  - "Spike bubblewrap confinement on Linux: fixture, minimal bind set, isolation assertions, lifecycle, diagnostics, portability matrix (T-008) and cost. This is the current ADR"
  - "Productionise the Linux isolation module: typed view to argv, invariants, version floor, golden argv, fail closed"
  - "Add the Nix devShell provider: host-side activation capture, exec wrapper, store write model, private workspace and snapshots"
  - "Lift the macOS helper from the Darwin spike into the same provider contract, reusing its SBPL renderer and lifecycle findings, with the deprecation risk documented"
  - "Refactor the provider interface around environments: exec, spawn, file transfer, capability declaration and per-provider configuration"
  - "Reach parity: ports, snapshot integration, CLI and MCP surface, documentation, and the platform risk of the macOS primitive"
